# Geneva-style clang-tidy baseline (C++23; checked against clang-tidy 23.1.6)
#
# This file complements .clang-format. It focuses on rules that clang-tidy can
# check reasonably well. Several Geneva coding conventions require custom
# checks or human review; see the notes at the end.
#
# This is a modernized version of the original Geneva-style configuration.
# The Geneva Coding Conventions are ~10 years old, written before C++11 was
# widespread. Where the conventions reference outdated practice (e.g.
# boost::uint32_t, omitting throw() specs), this config takes a pragmatic
# modern stance:
#
# - boost::uint32_t etc. are NOT enforced; std::uint32_t from <cstdint> is the
#   modern equivalent. The "use sized integer types" spirit is preserved.
# - The convention "omit throw() in member function declarations" is folded
#   into a noexcept-aware modern style; we let modernize-use-noexcept guide.
# - C++20 features (concepts, ranges, span, format) are encouraged but not
#   forced, to avoid disruptive changes in legacy code.
#
# Two checks are off because of what clang-tidy 21 and 22 brought:
# - cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-avoid-unchecked-container-access (new) reports
#   every operator[] on a container. Geneva indexes its containers on purpose; the
#   related pro-bounds checks are off for the same reason.
# - bugprone-throwing-static-initialization is the new name of cert-err58-cpp,
#   which was off already; the old name no longer silences the new one.
#
# Usage:
#   clang-tidy -p build path/to/file.cpp
#
# CLion and VS Code can pick this file up when clang-tidy is enabled.
#
# The measure is clang-tidy 23.1.6 (CLion is pointed at the same binary). What 23
# changed, and the reason for each line below that differs from the 22 version
# (a comment cannot stand inside the Checks list, so the reasons are here):
# - hicpp-exception-baseclass, hicpp-multiway-paths-covered and hicpp-signed-bitwise
#   are gone from 23; bugprone-* enables their successors (bugprone-std-exception-
#   baseclass, bugprone-unhandled-code-paths, bugprone-signed-bitwise).
# - One name per check. In 23 every cert-* check, and several cppcoreguidelines-,
#   google- and performance- checks, are second names (aliases) of a check of another
#   family. With both names on, a NOLINT that names one leaves the other one reporting,
#   and an option set under one name does not reach the other (the narrowing option
#   below was lost that way). So each check is enabled under one name only, the one of
#   its own family:
#   - cert-* is no longer enabled as a glob. Its checks run under their own names,
#     which bugprone-*, misc-*, modernize-*, performance-*, concurrency-* and
#     readability-* enable (cert-env33-c is bugprone-command-processor, cert-err60-cpp
#     bugprone-exception-copy-constructor-throws, cert-oop54-cpp
#     bugprone-unhandled-self-assignment, ...). Kept: cert-err33-c, which checks its
#     own list of C functions (fclose, setvbuf, ...) that bugprone-unused-return-value
#     does not; and four misc- checks that only their cert name had enabled, now named
#     below (misc-predictable-rand, misc-new-delete-overloads,
#     misc-anonymous-namespace-in-header, misc-non-copyable-objects).
#   - Off as second names, the first name in brackets:
#     -cppcoreguidelines-narrowing-conversions   (bugprone-narrowing-conversions)
#     -cppcoreguidelines-macro-to-enum           (modernize-macro-to-enum)
#     -cppcoreguidelines-use-default-member-init (modernize-use-default-member-init)
#     -cppcoreguidelines-explicit-constructor    (google-explicit-constructor, whose
#                                                 name the NOLINT comments use)
#     -cppcoreguidelines-noexcept-move-operations (performance-noexcept-move-constructor)
#     -cppcoreguidelines-noexcept-destructor     (performance-noexcept-destructor)
#     -cppcoreguidelines-noexcept-swap           (performance-noexcept-swap)
#     -cppcoreguidelines-explicit-virtual-functions (modernize-use-override, whose
#                                                 options are set below)
#     -google-readability-casting                (modernize-avoid-c-style-cast)
#     -performance-faster-string-find            (performance-prefer-single-char-overloads)
# - -cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory: ownership is held by unique_ptr/shared_ptr and
#   checked by modernize-make-unique/-shared (Inv 21); gsl::owner is not used. What
#   it reports are the deliberate raw pointers: clone_()'s raw return (Inv 21), the
#   never-destroyed registries, and the void* of the C-ABI module entry points.
# - -readability-trailing-comma (new in 23): the code writes no trailing comma (92 of
#   92 multi-line enums); layout belongs to clang-format (CODING-STYLE 5).
# - -readability-math-missing-parentheses: a * b + c is read by the usual precedence;
#   the parentheses it asks for clutter the formulas the code implements.
# - -clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange: it reports the deliberate
#   out-of-range casts of tests and casts inside Boost and Catch2 headers, which the
#   header filter does not reach.
# - -clang-analyzer-optin.performance.Padding: it reports padding in classes made once
#   per process (GLauncherT); the order of members follows their meaning.
# - modernize-use-string-view stays on: a function that returns only literals returns
#   std::string_view. A virtual interface (the getName() of the consumer providers)
#   keeps std::string with a NOLINT and its reason: an override that builds its name
#   would return a dangling view.

---
Checks: >-
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-crtp-constructor-accessibility,
  -bugprone-throwing-static-initialization,
  cert-err33-c,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  -clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange,
  -clang-analyzer-optin.performance.Padding,
  concurrency-*,
  cppcoreguidelines-*,
  -cppcoreguidelines-narrowing-conversions,
  -cppcoreguidelines-macro-to-enum,
  -cppcoreguidelines-use-default-member-init,
  -cppcoreguidelines-explicit-constructor,
  -cppcoreguidelines-noexcept-move-operations,
  -cppcoreguidelines-noexcept-destructor,
  -cppcoreguidelines-noexcept-swap,
  -cppcoreguidelines-explicit-virtual-functions,
  -cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-magic-numbers,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-array-to-pointer-decay,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-pointer-arithmetic,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-constant-array-index,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-avoid-unchecked-container-access,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-vararg,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-c-arrays,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-reinterpret-cast,
  -cppcoreguidelines-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-const-or-ref-data-members,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-do-while,
  -cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor,
  -google-build-using-namespace,
  google-explicit-constructor,
  google-readability-namespace-comments,
  misc-confusable-identifiers,
  misc-const-correctness,
  misc-definitions-in-headers,
  misc-anonymous-namespace-in-header,
  misc-new-delete-overloads,
  misc-non-copyable-objects,
  misc-predictable-rand,
  misc-misleading-bidirectional,
  misc-misleading-identifier,
  misc-misplaced-const,
  misc-redundant-expression,
  misc-static-assert,
  misc-throw-by-value-catch-by-reference,
  misc-unused-alias-decls,
  misc-unused-parameters,
  misc-unused-using-decls,
  modernize-*,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -modernize-avoid-c-arrays,
  performance-*,
  -performance-enum-size,
  -performance-faster-string-find,
  portability-*,
  -portability-avoid-pragma-once,
  -portability-template-virtual-member-function,
  readability-*,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-named-parameter,
  -readability-uppercase-literal-suffix,
  -readability-convert-member-functions-to-static,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-trailing-comma,
  -readability-math-missing-parentheses

WarningsAsErrors: ''
# Restrict header warnings to Geneva's own headers; this avoids noise from
# Boost, system, and third-party headers that appear in the same TU.
HeaderFilterRegex: '.*/include/(common|hap|dietrich|courtier|geneva|weft)/.*'
FormatStyle: file
UseColor: true

CheckOptions:
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Brace style for control flow.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The Geneva conventions show several examples WITHOUT braces around
  # single-statement bodies (e.g. setMyClassVariable). Setting
  # ShortStatementLines: '1' tolerates the documented one-line form,
  # while still requiring braces when the body wraps.
  readability-braces-around-statements.ShortStatementLines: '1'

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Namespace closing comments.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Geneva uses `} /* namespace X */`. clang-tidy can only generate
  # `// namespace X` style, so we leave it as a soft warning rather
  # than a strict fixer; team review handles the exact spelling.
  google-readability-namespace-comments.ShortNamespaceLines: '1'
  google-readability-namespace-comments.SpacesBeforeComments: '1'

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Header / implementation parameter consistency.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # A header declaration uses the same parameter names as the definition;
  # this check reports a parameter renamed in one place but not the other.
  readability-inconsistent-declaration-parameter-name.Strict: 'true'

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Identifier naming.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # CODING-STYLE 4 (the rules for members, namespaces, globals and the exempt names:
  # maintainer decisions 2026-11-27). The 'G' prefix and 'T' suffix of template
  # classes are NOT enforced because the convention says "usually" (4.3).
  readability-identifier-naming.FunctionCase: camelBack
  readability-identifier-naming.MethodCase: camelBack
  # A mutable global, static or thread_local variable is snake_case; a g_ / t_
  # prefix is allowed (lower_case accepts it), not required. Variable is the kind that
  # remains for them after the specific kinds below.
  readability-identifier-naming.VariableCase: lower_case
  # A static data member is not a "member" to clang-tidy. A private one is named as
  # a private member (snake_case_); lower_case cannot express the trailing underscore,
  # hence the regexp.
  readability-identifier-naming.ClassMemberCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.ClassMemberIgnoredRegexp: '^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*_$'
  # Local variables and function parameters are snake_case; private and protected
  # members are snake_case with a trailing '_'.
  readability-identifier-naming.ParameterCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.LocalVariableCase: lower_case
  # A public member (of the plain settings, spec and C-ABI structs) is snake_case
  # without the trailing underscore, as most of them are written.
  readability-identifier-naming.MemberCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.PublicMemberCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberSuffix: _
  readability-identifier-naming.ProtectedMemberCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.ProtectedMemberSuffix: _
  readability-identifier-naming.ClassCase: CamelCase
  readability-identifier-naming.StructCase: CamelCase
  # Names that the standard library fixes: a distribution's nested param_type (the
  # RandomNumberDistribution requirement) and the std-style is_... type traits.
  readability-identifier-naming.ClassIgnoredRegexp: '^param_type$'
  readability-identifier-naming.StructIgnoredRegexp: '^(param_type|is_[a-z0-9_]+)$'
  readability-identifier-naming.UnionCase: CamelCase
  readability-identifier-naming.EnumCase: CamelCase
  # Geneva enum constants are UPPER_CASE: TEXT, XML, BINARY, LOWERISBETTER, etc.
  readability-identifier-naming.EnumConstantCase: UPPER_CASE
  readability-identifier-naming.ScopedEnumConstantCase: UPPER_CASE
  # Geneva 5.B / §4 STL carve-out: GContainerT (and its GPodContainerT /
  # GPtrContainerT typedefs) deliberately mirror the std:: container
  # surface — value_type, const_iterator, size_type, difference_type,
  # reverse_iterator, etc. Forcing CamelCase here would break the STL
  # contract, so type aliases and typedefs accept any case.
  readability-identifier-naming.TypeAliasCase: aNy_CasE
  readability-identifier-naming.TypedefCase: aNy_CasE
  # Template parameters (maintainer decision 2026-11-15): a type or template template
  # parameter is snake_case ending in '_type' (archive_type, derived_type); a single
  # capital letter, with an 's' for a pack (T, U, F, Ts), stays allowed for a parameter
  # without a specific role. A non-type parameter is UPPER_CASE, as the other constants
  # (N, N_DIM, CAP). A constrained type parameter (template <std::integral int_type>)
  # counts as a type parameter. The general TemplateParameterCase is not set: the three
  # specific options cover every kind.
  readability-identifier-naming.TypeTemplateParameterCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.TypeTemplateParameterSuffix: _type
  readability-identifier-naming.TypeTemplateParameterIgnoredRegexp: '^[A-Z]s?$'
  readability-identifier-naming.TemplateTemplateParameterCase: lower_case
  readability-identifier-naming.TemplateTemplateParameterSuffix: _type
  readability-identifier-naming.TemplateTemplateParameterIgnoredRegexp: '^[A-Z]s?$'
  readability-identifier-naming.ValueTemplateParameterCase: UPPER_CASE
  readability-identifier-naming.ConceptCase: CamelCase
  # Every namespace is CamelCase, a namespace alias (clang-tidy reports it as
  # a namespace) and the private namespace (Detail) as well; no exemption.
  readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceCase: CamelCase
  readability-identifier-naming.MacroDefinitionCase: UPPER_CASE
  readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantCase: UPPER_CASE
  # An object kept only for what its constructor does at start-up (the
  # g_..._registrant of each algorithm, a lifecycle guard) is named as a variable,
  # not as a value constant.
  readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantIgnoredRegexp: '^g_[a-z0-9_]+$'
  # UPPER_CASE is for values. An object that is changed behind a const pointer
  # (static T *const inst = new T) is a variable and snake_case, but clang-tidy files
  # every const function-local static under StaticConstant and cannot tell the two
  # apart; such a declaration carries a NOLINT with that reason.
  readability-identifier-naming.StaticConstantCase: UPPER_CASE
  readability-identifier-naming.ClassConstantCase: UPPER_CASE
  readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariableCase: UPPER_CASE
  # Names a user's class must supply, or that mirror the standard library, follow
  # their contract: class_name (GReflectiveInterfaceT), oa_mnemonic and
  # oa_algorithm_name (GOAPlugin), is_saving / is_loading of an archive, and the
  # std-style ..._v variable templates a user specializes (enum_names_v,
  # numeric_enum_values_v), and default_seed, the name the standard's random
  # engines give their default seed (the xoshiro engines of hap).
  readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariableIgnoredRegexp: '^(class_name|oa_mnemonic|oa_algorithm_name|is_saving|is_loading|default_seed|[a-z][a-z0-9_]*_v)$'
  readability-identifier-naming.GlobalFunctionCase: camelBack
  # A free function with the prefix gem_ is snake_case: the C-ABI entry points of a
  # module (gem_module_manifest) and the ADL hooks of weft (gem_archive_serialize) are
  # found by a name fixed outside C++ name lookup. to_string and from_string mirror
  # std::to_string and keep its form.
  readability-identifier-naming.GlobalFunctionIgnoredRegexp: '^(gem_[a-z0-9_]+|to_string|from_string)$'
  readability-identifier-naming.FunctionIgnoredRegexp: '^(gem_[a-z0-9_]+|to_string|from_string)$'
  readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprFunctionCase: camelBack
  readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprFunctionIgnoredRegexp: '^gem_[a-z0-9_]+$'
  readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprMethodCase: camelBack
  # The carve-out of CODING-STYLE 4.1 holds for a constexpr method as well (clang-tidy
  # gives it a kind of its own, which MethodIgnoredRegexp does not reach).
  readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprMethodIgnoredRegexp: '.*_.*'
  # Geneva 5.B / §4 STL carve-out: exempt the STL-like container method
  # surface from the camelBack rule, plus any method containing an underscore
  # (the STL names push_back/pop_back/emplace_back/shrink_to_fit/max_size, and
  # Geneva's trailing-underscore private-virtual pattern, e.g. load_, plus
  # snake_case internal helpers such as compare_base).
  # NOTE: clang-tidy matches *IgnoredRegexp against the WHOLE identifier, so the
  # exemption must be a full-string pattern ('.*_.*'), not a bare '_' (which
  # would only match the identifier literally named "_").
  readability-identifier-naming.MethodIgnoredRegexp: '.*_.*'

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Modernization knobs.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  modernize-use-override.AllowOverrideAndFinal: 'true'
  modernize-use-override.IgnoreDestructors: 'false'
  modernize-use-default-member-init.UseAssignment: 'true'
  modernize-use-emplace.IgnoreImplicitConstructors: 'true'
  modernize-use-auto.MinTypeNameLength: '5'
  modernize-loop-convert.MinConfidence: reasonable
  modernize-pass-by-value.IncludeStyle: llvm
  modernize-replace-auto-ptr.IncludeStyle: llvm
  modernize-use-nullptr.NullMacros: 'NULL'
  modernize-use-noexcept.ReplacementString: 'noexcept'
  # noexcept(false) annotations are almost never useful and generate noise.
  modernize-use-noexcept.UseNoexceptFalse: 'false'

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Bugprone / cppcoreguidelines tuning.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # No magic numbers in production code, but allow common literals 0/1/-1
  # in test code without complaining (avoids alarm fatigue).
  cppcoreguidelines-init-variables.IncludeStyle: llvm

  # Narrowing conversions: flag genuine *width* truncation only. Conversions
  # between types of equal bit width -- size_t -> ptrdiff_t in iterator
  # arithmetic (`begin() + n`), int -> float for image dimensions -- are
  # sign/precision concerns rather than truncation: no Geneva container comes
  # anywhere near PTRDIFF_MAX. Leaving them enabled produced 64 such reports
  # that buried the findings that matter, namely the 64->32 size_t -> int
  # truncations at the MPI message-count arguments. Those still warn.
  bugprone-narrowing-conversions.WarnOnEquivalentBitWidth: 'false'

  # A shift count or mask written as a positive literal (x >> 11, v & 0xFF) is not a
  # signed operand in any sense that matters; the check reported those on unsigned x.
  bugprone-signed-bitwise.IgnorePositiveIntegerLiterals: 'true'

  # An exception that leaves main() is reported by the terminate handler of
  # Gem::Common::installFatalReport() (message and exit code 1), which every program
  # installs: that is the designed end of an unhandled error, not an escape.
  bugprone-exception-escape.CheckMain: 'false'

  # optional::value() checks and throws, so it is not an unchecked access; the check
  # does not see that checkThat() throws, and value() after it is the clean spelling.
  bugprone-unchecked-optional-access.IgnoreValueCalls: 'true'

  # std::from_chars takes the end pointer, so data() needs no terminating zero there.
  bugprone-suspicious-stringview-data-usage.AllowedCallees: '::std::from_chars'

  cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions.AllowSoleDefaultDtor: 'true'
  cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions.AllowMissingMoveFunctions: 'true'
  cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions.AllowMissingMoveFunctionsWhenCopyIsDeleted: 'true'

  # Macros should be avoided per Geneva guideline "function macros should
  # be avoided at all costs" and "defines may not be used for specific
  # values". cppcoreguidelines-macro-usage flags both.
  # Allowed: the G_CALLABLE device annotation, the GEM_* macros of the collection
  # (build guards, config macros, GEM_DEPRECATED, GEM_DEBUG_CHECK -- only a macro
  # can leave its condition unevaluated -- and GEM_LOGEXIT, which captures the
  # source location at the exit site) and the geneva library's GENEVA_* macros.
  cppcoreguidelines-macro-usage.AllowedRegexp: '^(G_.*|GEM_.*|GENEVA_.*)$'

  # Performance.
  performance-for-range-copy.WarnOnAllAutoCopies: 'true'
  performance-unnecessary-value-param.AllowedTypes: ''
  performance-inefficient-string-concatenation.StrictMode: 'true'

  # Misc.
  misc-throw-by-value-catch-by-reference.WarnOnLargeObject: 'true'
  misc-throw-by-value-catch-by-reference.MaxSize: '64'

  # Readability.
  readability-implicit-bool-conversion.AllowIntegerConditions: 'true'
  readability-implicit-bool-conversion.AllowPointerConditions: 'true'
  # Function size / complexity (Clean Code "functions stay small, do one thing";
  # C++ Core Guidelines F.2/F.3). These checks are ENABLED; the thresholds below are
  # a starting point -- a genuinely irreducible hot kernel gets a NOLINT with a
  # recorded reason (cf. Invariant 14) rather than a forced, worse split.
  readability-function-size.LineThreshold: '100'
  readability-function-size.StatementThreshold: '50'
  readability-function-size.BranchThreshold: '15'
  readability-function-size.ParameterThreshold: '8'
  readability-function-size.NestingThreshold: '5'
  # Cognitive complexity: start lenient (LLVM default is 25) so only the gnarliest
  # functions are flagged; tighten toward 25 as the code is groomed.
  readability-function-cognitive-complexity.Threshold: '40'
  # A Catch2 TEST_CASE is a list of REQUIRE / CHECK macros; their expansions were
  # counted as branches (63 of the 69 reports were test cases).
  readability-function-cognitive-complexity.IgnoreMacros: 'true'
  readability-function-size.IgnoreMacros: 'true'

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules NOT covered by stock clang-tidy, or only partially:
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# - Doxygen comments before every file, class, function and variable.
#   (Use a Doxygen completeness checker or pre-commit hook instead.)
# - Exact file header / license block.
#   (Use a custom regex script in CI.)
# - No 'using namespace' in a header, also not in a function body.
#   (google-build-using-namespace is off: it would also flag the uses
#    allowed in the file of main() and in tests.)
# - Prefer 'typename' over 'class' in template parameter lists.
#   (modernize-use-using covers part of this; full check requires custom.)
# - Template classes / structs ending with 'T' only.
# - Interface classes ending with 'I' only.
# - Core Geneva classes starting with 'G' only where applicable.
#   (These three could be added via readability-identifier-naming
#    .ClassPrefix / .ClassSuffix, but cause too many false positives
#    in code that interfaces with external libraries.)
# - File extension and one-main-class-per-file rules.
#   (Filesystem-level check, outside of clang-tidy's scope.)
#
# Modernization beyond the original conventions:
# - std::uint32_t etc. (from <cstdint>) replace boost::uint32_t.
# - noexcept replaces throw() specifications.
# - Macros for constants are flagged (cppcoreguidelines-macro-usage).
# - A smart pointer built from a naked new is flagged (modernize-make-shared,
#   modernize-make-unique; named here because CODING-STYLE.md relies on them).
# - C-style casts are flagged (modernize-avoid-c-style-cast, which clang-tidy 23
#   also answers to as google-readability-casting, and cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-cstyle-cast).
...
